WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.760
We have explored the method&nbsp;
of string compression called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.240
Burrows-Wheeler Transform that's going to let&nbsp;
us convert the repeats of a genome into runs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.920
so that we can apply some other&nbsp;
compression, such as run length encoding.&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:22.640
There is a strange observation in Burrows-Wheeler&nbsp;
Transform. For example, if we consider the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.720
string “PANAMABANANAS”, you will notice&nbsp;
that, if we start with the last column,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:32.720
we get the first column for free. For example,&nbsp;
the six occurrences of “As” is highlighted here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.890
In the first column, the 6 occurrences of&nbsp;
“As” also appear in the last column. Here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:38.800
we have highlighted where those “As” occur in sort&nbsp;
of these cyclic representation of “PANAMABANANAS”.&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.800
If we look at the first “A” in the first&nbsp;
column and the first “A” in the last column,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.360
you'll notice that these two “As” correspond to&nbsp;
the same position in the string “PANAMABANANAS”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.800
which is the third “A” in “PANAMABANANAS”. You can&nbsp;
see that this pattern is consistent throughout.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.080
We can look at the “Ns” too. It's not&nbsp;
something that's particular to “A”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.840
it's for any symbol. So if we look at the “Ns”,&nbsp;
the first appearance of “N” in the last column and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.480
the first appearance of “N” in the first column,&nbsp;
they both correspond to the highlighted “Ns” here.&nbsp;

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.840
We will try to verify whether this property&nbsp;
of Burrows-Wheeler Transform hold in general.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:18.000
If we look at the six columns that&nbsp;
start with an “A” and they're sorted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:24.640
If we chop off an “A”, the rows are still sorted.&nbsp;
This is because they all start with “A” and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:30.160
remainder of the strings are still sorted. Now,&nbsp;
if we add this “A” back to the end, then those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.160
strings should still be sorted because all we&nbsp;
did was add an “A” to the end of all of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:40.160
So we've essentially done a cyclic rotation of our&nbsp;
six original strings where we just took the “A”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:45.360
and put it on the end, and those strings&nbsp;
remain sorted. What that means is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.480
if we look for those cyclic rotations back in the&nbsp;
original Burrows-Wheeler matrix wherever they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:58.320
they should still maintain the same ordering.
In short, the k-th occurrence of any symbol in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:03.200
First Column and the k-th occurrence of any symbol&nbsp;
in the final column of the Burrows-Wheeler matrix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.800
correspond to the exact same position of that&nbsp;
symbol in the original genome. So you can index&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:14.720
your occurrences of each symbol in the First and&nbsp;
Last Column. For example, the index of the sixth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.800
“A” here in the first column is corresponding&nbsp;
to the sixth “A” here in the last column.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:26.560
Same for the “Ns”. Index n1, n2 and n3 in&nbsp;
the first column here correspond to n1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:32.880
n2 and n3 in the last column here.
How does this relate to pattern matching? Here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:39.200
is an example. Presumably we want to match the&nbsp;
string “ANA” to the genome string “PANAMABANANAS”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.720
we start from the last character, “A”. We look&nbsp;
in the first column and we can identify on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:51.200
rows that “A” appear. Then, the next character&nbsp;
“N”. We will look at the last column and our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:57.920
search now limit to the string with N1, N2, and&nbsp;
N3. Using the first last property, we can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:05.200
identify where N1, N2, N3 appears in the first&nbsp;
column. So now, we have identified two letters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:12.720
The next is “A”. We just go back, and find&nbsp;
all “A”. Using the first last property,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:19.360
and we can again know where the A3, A4, A5 appear&nbsp;
in the first column. In this case, the string&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:25.520
“ANA” appears three times, in the genome string.
In the previous example, we show that we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:30.960
identify which pattern match to the genome&nbsp;
using Burrows-Wheeler Transform. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.600
for multiple pattern matching problem we also need&nbsp;
to find where is the pattern matches happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.960
So the question is, can we use Burrows-Wheeler&nbsp;
Transform to reveal these positions?

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.440
If we go back to our example, we know that “ANA”&nbsp;
occurs 3 times in our genome, “PANAMABANANAS”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:52.160
If we're using Burrows-Wheeler, we&nbsp;
only have the first and the last column&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:55.120
and we don't know where it is that&nbsp;
these pattern matches are occurring.&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:02.000
So, we'll use a data selector called a suffix&nbsp;
array. To do this, we are going to identify the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:07.200
starting position of each suffix at the beginning&nbsp;
of a row. Each row of the Burrows-Wheeler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:13.840
transform corresponds to a cyclic rotation of our&nbsp;
original string. So, if it's a cyclic rotation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.560
then we can walk to some point and reach the&nbsp;
$ and that walking corresponds to a suffix.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.800
For example, the first row is just the $ sign,&nbsp;
so it is the suffix at the end of the string&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:31.520
and that's in position 13, so we put a 13 on that&nbsp;
row. The next rows begins with “A” “BANANAS” and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:37.040
the “A” “BANANAS” is in position 5 of the original&nbsp;
string, so we put a 5 in the second element of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:42.560
suffix array. If we go through this, we will&nbsp;
be able to construct an entire suffix array.&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:48.560
How is that helpful to find the matches?&nbsp;
Well, if we look at the three occurrences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.040
“ANA” that we found, we can identify the&nbsp;
corresponding elements in the suffix array.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:05:04.480
It tells us that “ANA” occurs at&nbsp;
positions 1, 7, and 9 of “PANAMABANANAS”.

